From 48a142fb8a2ace74f517fa368ce9f337a7d47156 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: dinadur <59811564+dinadur@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 8 Oct 2026 18:15:42 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] B70: route natural product, fact-check and travel phrasings

The 2026-10-08 routing sweep found that most everyday phrasings reached no
research route: product 1 of 12, generic 3 of 10, travel 3 of 5. Unrouted
turns get no controller custody.

- Product: a natural shopping ask also selects the route. Asks are "what's /
  which / should I / is the ... or ... better / find me / recommend / pick /
  choose / I need / I'm looking for / any recommendations". The ask must say
  best, good, top, recommend or should I get/buy, and name a product signal:
  a price or budget, a buying verb, a model-like token, or a common consumer
  product category. "X vs Y" needs a model-like token; "compare" needs a
  model token, price or buying verb. Non-product superlatives (best way to /
  time / restaurant / order ...), "this paragraph"-style objects, "not product
  research" and "do not use tools" never match.
- Generic: "look into", "find out", "verify", "fact-check", "check
  whether/what/if/how/when" at the start, and "is it true", route without
  source wording; "check the official docs/release notes" counts as intent.
  A plain "Research X" with no source or save wording is unchanged, by design.
  "Do not use tools" never routes.
- Travel: "I'm going/heading/flying/driving to <place>" is destination
  context; "things to do", "weekend in", "for N days" and "N-day(s) in" are
  trip cues.

tests/agent/test_route_phrasing_corpus.py holds 97 phrasings with expected
routes, including chat negatives. Two later sets of 18 and 20 untuned
phrasings scored 18/18 and 19/20. Of the 100 canary-suite prompts only one
changed route: a Kindle/Kobo comparison with a budget, now product.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
